    "prompt": "INPUTS \n@image1 — the user’s photo, used only for the person’s appearance. \n@video1 — the depth-map video, used for motion, timing, spatial layout and camera choreography.\n\nOUTPUT Create a photorealistic vertical 9:16 video. Match the exact duration and playback speed of @video1. One continuous shot, with no additional cuts, introductory sequence or ending extension. \n\nREFERENCE PRIORITY \n@image1 defines the person’s identity: facial features, skin tone, apparent age, hairstyle, facial hair and visible clothing. Preserve their recognizable appearance throughout, especially during the final close-up. Do not copy the photograph’s background, pose or expression. @video1 defines the flight trajectory, body movements, gesture timing, camera movement, framing, perspective and foreground occlusions. Interpret the depth map as spatial guidance, never as the finished appearance. Do not transfer the source performer’s identity, hair, makeup or costume. \n\nACTION AND CAMERA \nThe person is already flying at high speed on a wooden broomstick in the opening frame. Follow the complete action and camera trajectory in @video1, preserving the timing of every movement. Reproduce the flight beside the camera, the approach, the forward-reaching arm and the transition into the final close-up. Keep acceleration and changes in distance continuous and physically readable. Do not add extra tricks, gestures or a mounting sequence. During the camera-grab action, show the person’s arm extending naturally from their shoulder toward the lens. They abruptly grasp the unseen recording device, and the viewpoint jolts as they pull it toward themselves. Brief partial occlusion by their fingers is acceptable only where consistent with the depth reference. This is the SAME camera throughout. No cut to a second camera, no device handover and no extra hand from the original camera operator. Do not show a phone or camera body: the audience continues seeing through its lens. After taking the camera, the person holds it at arm’s length facing themselves while continuing the flight, following the reference framing and movement. Preserve their recognizable face as it becomes larger in the frame. \n\nFINAL PERFORMANCE \nIn the final close-up, the person looks directly into the lens, establishing unmistakable eye contact with the viewer. Their expression becomes playful, confident and slightly mischievous. They clearly say, exactly once: “Why so serious?” Deliver the line in natural English with a lightly sarcastic, ironic and subtly ominous tone. Use a natural voice appropriate to the depicted person, without imitating a particular actor. Place the line within the final close-up, aligned with the source performance’s visible mouth movement where available. Keep speech speed natural and finish the entire phrase before the video ends. Accurate lip synchronization, believable jaw movement and restrained facial animation. After the line, laugh villainously before the video ends. \n\nIDENTITY AND CLOTHING \nRetarget the source movement to the person’s own proportions while maintaining the choreography. Preserve identity through profile views, rapid motion, partial hand occlusion and close-ups. Keep the visible outfit from @image1 consistent throughout. Plausibly complete clothing and footwear outside the photograph’s crop. Do not automatically add a witch hat, cape, theatrical makeup, gloves or a fantasy costume. Treat the source silhouette as a motion guide: do not turn the original performer’s coat tails or hair into stray ropes, extra clothing or elongated hair on the new person. \n\nPHYSICS AND DEPTH Use one broom with a continuous wooden shaft and a firmly secured bundle of bristles. Maintain believable seated contact and hand grips wherever shown in the reference. The body and broom bank together with coherent balance and inertia. Preserve consistent anatomy and limb lengths. The reaching hand becomes larger through perspective as it approaches the lens, not by changing its physical size. Correct finger articulation and natural wrist, elbow and shoulder movement. Maintain the reference depth ordering and occlusions. No intersecting limbs, detached hands, sliding grips, duplicated broomsticks or floating body separated from the broom. Hair and loose fabric respond naturally to the direction and speed of airflow. \n\nENVIRONMENT AND IMAGE QUALITY \nRender the depth scene in full color with realistic materials and soft natural daylight. Reconstruct visible foreground structures and environmental geometry from @video1. Where the reference depicts a car window and interior, preserve their spatial placement and occlusion of the outdoor scene. Use a natural roadside landscape and atmospheric sky consistent with the flight and camera path. Follow any transition from the initial viewpoint into open air exactly as indicated by the reference. The person flies through a real-looking environment, never through a visible depth mask, screen or portal. Detailed skin, stable facial features, realistic fabric, hair and wood. Consistent lighting and perspective throughout. Keep the face readable, with restrained motion blur appropriate to movement. No flickering, identity drift, text, subtitles, logos or watermarks. \n\nAUDIO \nThe only added audio is the clearly spoken final line: “Why so serious?” followed by the person’s villainous laughter. No music, additional dialogue, wind sounds or sound effects.\n\n"
