    "prompt": "TASK & FORMAT\nRecreate @video1 as a photorealistic vertical 9:16 video, preserving the original duration, shot structure, camera movement, framing, timing, environment, basketball actions, and choreography.\n\nReplace the green character with the character from @image1.\nReplace the red character with the character from @image2.\n\nREFERENCES\n@video1 = base motion, timing, choreography, gesture timing, environment, basketball action, camera behavior, and scene structure reference.\n@image1 = replacement identity for the original green character.\n@image2 = replacement identity for the original red character.\n\nROLE ASSIGNMENT\nThe original green character in @video1 must be fully replaced by the character shown in @image1.\nThe original red character in @video1 must be fully replaced by the character shown in @image2.\n\nCHARACTER REPLACEMENT\nPreserve the recognizable identity of @image1 throughout the entire video:\nface or head design, body shape, anatomy, proportions, fur or skin, outfit if present, colors, textures, accessories, and distinctive features.\n\nPreserve the recognizable identity of @image2 throughout the entire video:\nface or head design, body shape, anatomy, proportions, fur or skin, outfit if present, colors, textures, accessories, and distinctive features.\n\nDo not keep any visual traits from the green or red placeholder characters.\nDo not swap identities.\nDo not let the characters morph or drift.\n\nCRITICAL MOTION RULE\nThis is the most important requirement:\npreserve the original motion, gesture timing, body mechanics, and action choreography from @video1 as closely as possible.\n\nThe replacement for the green character must perform the exact same role, movement sequence, basketball actions, and expressive gestures as the original green character.\n\nThe replacement for the red character must perform the exact same role, movement sequence, defensive reactions, and expressive gestures as the original red character.\n\nPreserve:\n- dribbling and ball handling\n- running path and footwork\n- defensive stance and reactions\n- jumps and landing timing\n- arm movement timing\n- hand or paw gesture timing\n- body turns and posture changes\n- final reaction gestures\n- relative screen positions and interaction timing\n\nANATOMY ADAPTATION RULE\nIf @image1 or @image2 is a non-human character, creature, or animal, adapt the motion naturally to that anatomy while preserving the same intent, timing, rhythm, and scene function.\n\nFor animals:\n- preserve the same role and action timing\n- translate hand gestures into the closest believable anatomical equivalent\n- keep the same expressive intent and comedic beat\n- maintain the same relative body movement and scene choreography\n\nExamples:\n- a human finger gesture may become a paw gesture, head gesture, wing gesture, or equivalent expressive motion\n- a human defensive stance may become an animal’s natural alert or blocking posture\n- a human jump or run should be adapted naturally to the animal’s locomotion while still matching the timing and trajectory as closely as possible\n\nEven with anatomy adaptation, the movement rhythm and action sequence must remain highly faithful to @video1.\n\nSCENE\nPreserve the exact outdoor basketball court setting from @video1:\nnight court, basketball hoop, backboard, asphalt ground, chain-link fence, background crowd, overhead lights, red dramatic sky, and the same spatial arrangement.\n\nDo not change the environment, prop placement, crowd positioning, or lighting setup.\n\nACTION FLOW\nMatch the original sequence exactly.\n\nBeginning:\nThe replacement character from @image1 takes the role of the original green character.\nThe replacement character from @image2 takes the role of the original red character.\n\nMiddle:\nThe @image1 character performs the same offensive basketball movements, feints, dribbling, and approach timing.\nThe @image2 character performs the same defensive movements, tracking, pursuit, and reactions.\n\nShot and reaction:\nPreserve the same jump timing, ball release timing, follow-through, and post-action reaction timing.\n\nIf the original green character makes a rude or taunting gesture after the play, the @image1 replacement must perform the closest equivalent gesture for its anatomy at the same moment and with the same attitude.\n\nIf the original red character performs a frustrated reaction or facepalm-like gesture, the @image2 replacement must perform the closest equivalent reaction for its anatomy at the same moment and with the same emotional intent.\n\nCAMERA\nPreserve the original camera choreography from @video1:\nsame shot sequence, same framing, same lens feel, same perspective, same movement, and same pace.\n\nDo not redesign the scene.\nDo not add new shots.\nDo not invent new camera angles.\n\nCONTINUITY LOCKS\nIdentity lock:\n@image1 remains the same character in every frame.\n@image2 remains the same character in every frame.\n\nMotion lock:\nAll major movement, gesture timing, and basketball choreography must follow @video1 closely.\n\nInteraction lock:\nBoth characters must interact with each other and the basketball in the same timing and spatial relationship as the original video.\n\nScene lock:\nPreserve the environment, hoop, court, crowd, sky, and overall composition.\n\nVISUAL STYLE\nPhotorealistic, cinematic, believable integration into the real scene.\nNatural lighting, realistic shadows, grounded body mechanics, realistic motion blur, and consistent scale.\n\nIf the replacement characters are animals, they must still feel physically believable and naturally integrated into the environment.\n\nAUDIO\nPreserve the original type of sound design:\nbasketball bounce, footsteps or equivalent movement sounds, court ambience, hoop and net interaction, and crowd reaction atmosphere.\n\nNo unnecessary music.\nNo exaggerated cartoon sound effects.\nNo unrelated dialogue.\n\nCONSTRAINTS\nNo identity drift.\nNo character swapping.\nNo major choreography changes.\nNo missing gestures.\nNo extra gestures that were not in the original.\nNo environment redesign.\nNo duplicated characters.\nNo anatomy glitches.\nNo extra limbs.\nNo text overlays.\nNo subtitles.\nNo watermarks.\n\nFINAL RESULT\nThe final video should closely recreate @video1, but the green and red placeholder characters are fully replaced by the characters from @image1 and @image2.\n\nAll timing, gestures, basketball actions, body movement, reactions, and scene choreography must remain as faithful as possible to the original.\n\nIf the replacement characters are animals or non-human beings, adapt the gestures naturally to their anatomy while preserving the original timing, intent, and role in the scene."
